Check if the kernel FIPS mode is enabled:
$ cat /proc/sys/crypto/fips_enabled
1

$ sysctl crypto.fips_enabled
crypto.fips_enabled = 1

NOTE: 1 indicates enabled, while 0 indicates disabled.


3. Using the OpenSSL FIPS Module in applications

Note that the old functions FIPS_mode() and FIPS_mode_set() are no longer present
so you must remove them from your application if you use them.

A simple approach to use FIPS module is to cause all applications that are using
OpenSSL to only use the FIPS module for cryptographic algorithms by default.

This approach can be done purely via configuration. As long as applications are
built and linked against OpenSSL 3.x and do not override the loading of the default
config file or its settings then they can automatically start using the FIPS module
without the need for any further code changes.

Steps:

a). Verify that the FIPS config file fipsmodule.cnf contains the correct info:

$ openssl fipsinstall -module /usr/lib64/ossl-modules/fips.so -in /usr/lib64/ssl-3/fipsmodule.cnf -verify
VERIFY PASSED

b). Edit the OpenSSL config file /usr/lib64/ssl-3/openssl.cnf:

  config_diagnostics = 1
  openssl_conf = openssl_init

  .include /usr/lib64/ssl-3/fipsmodule.cnf

  [openssl_init]
  providers = provider_sect
  alg_section = algorithm_sect

  [provider_sect]
  fips = fips_sect
  base = base_sect

  [base_sect]
  activate = 1

  [algorithm_sect]
  default_properties = fips=yes

c). Check if the fips module is loaded:

$ openssl list -providers
Providers:
  base
    name: OpenSSL Base Provider
    version: 3.5.2
    status: active
  fips
    name: OpenSSL FIPS Provider
    version: 3.5.2
    status: active

Any applications that use OpenSSL 3.x and are started after these changes
are made will start using only the FIPS module.

d). Setup OpenSSH server:

We need to set FIPS-compliant algorithms in Ciphers and MACs for OpenSSH sever.
Other algorithms will be disabled in FIPS mode.

For example, add the follwing lines to /etc/ssh/sshd_config:
  Ciphers aes256-ctr,aes192-ctr,aes128-ctr
  MACs hmac-sha2-512,hmac-sha2-256

$ ssh root@localhost
Last login: Tue May 13 15:42:50 2025 from 127.0.0.1


Another approach is selectively making applications use the FIPS module by
default It is possible to override the default OpenSSL config file to be used
via the OPENSSL_CONF environment variable. For example:

$ OPENSSL_CONF=/my/nondefault/openssl.cnf myapplication


See https://docs.openssl.org/3.5/man7/fips_module/ for more approaches.


